      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# TEST: EPS Run Multiplier - 2 images x 4 prompts = 8\n\n**Setup (one time):** copy `eps-cross-test-prompts.md` (ships beside this file) into your EPSNodes library folder - the one in Settings -> EPSNodes. That is the only prep.\n\n**Then just press Run.** No checkpoint, no lora, no GPU needed - it only moves images around.\n\n## What should happen\n\n**8 images** appear in your output folder, named by prompt:\n\n```\nNeon City_i1_t1_00001_.png      <- Image A\nGolden Hour_i1_t2_00001_.png    <- Image A\nInk Sketch_i1_t3_00001_.png     <- Image A\nPastel Studio_i1_t4_00001_.png  <- Image A\nNeon City_i2_t1_00001_.png      <- Image B\nGolden Hour_i2_t2_00001_.png    <- Image B\nInk Sketch_i2_t3_00001_.png     <- Image B\nPastel Studio_i2_t4_00001_.png  <- Image B\n```\n\nEach prompt name appears exactly **twice** (once per image), and the run token in the name says which: `i1` = Image A, `i2` = Image B (`tN` = the prompt). It is the token, not the `_00001_` counter, that tells the two apart. That is the fix: without EPS Run Multiplier you would get 4 images, three of them reusing the last picture.\n\n**Try it your way too:** swap the two Load Image nodes for your own, or replace the switcher with an **EPS Image Grid** set to *Emit* (wire its `image` output into the Run Multiplier's `image` input) - the count follows whatever is in the grid."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
